    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-47408",
    ":question/problem": "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{5}-2x$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=ax+b$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. Find the value of $a+b.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-2x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-2x)=10{x}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(1)=10(1)^{4}-2=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=0.$"
  },
