    ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-{x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,3).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=4x-{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-{x}^{3})=4-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=4-3({1}^{2})=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-1\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+1\\cdot y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
