    ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(2,2).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-16{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(2)=-\\frac{16}{(2)^{3}}=-\\frac{16}{8}=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-4 & =x-2 \\\\ x-2\\cdot y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
