    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342162",
    ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=6{x}^{2}-7x+4$ at the point $(0,4).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-7x+4.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-7x+4)=12x-7\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,4):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(0)=12(0)-7=-7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{7}(x-0) \\\\ 7(y-4) & =x \\\\ 7y-28 & =x \\\\ x-7\\cdot y & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
