    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342168",
    ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,-6).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.\n$$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
