    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-47412",
    ":question/problem": "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(1)=-\\frac{9}{(1)^{4}}=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$"
  },
