    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-47398",
    ":question/problem": "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m &={f}^{\u2032}(1)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(1)) \\\\ 4y-16 & =x-1 \\\\ x-4y & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=-4+(-15)=-19.$"
  }
